# PulseNSFW v2 — env placeholders. Do not commit real values.

Root note: the repo root `.env.local` belongs to the Neon CLI link (project
`steep-field-24680180`, PRODUCTION branch) — do not overwrite or commit it.
`v2/.env` is this folder's dev-env file (gitignored); keep the two explicit:

- root `.env.local`  — Neon CLI variables (remote credentials; never printed or committed here)
- `v2/.env`          — dev only (AGENT_* test keys, AGENT_DEV_STORE=memory, CONCIERGE_DEV_MOCK)

## Neon Postgres (production database; server-side only)

The app connects with the official Neon serverless driver over HTTP
(https://neon.com/docs/serverless/serverless-driver — accessed 2026-11-07).
Set the pooled or direct URL; one key, server-only.

# NEON_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>/<db>?sslmode=require
# (legacy alias also accepted: DATABASE_URL)

## Neon Auth — Managed Better Auth (honest until enabled)

Enable in the Neon Console (Branch → Auth → Enable), then copy the Auth URL
and set a 32+ char cookie secret. Until BOTH exist everything on the desk
stays in its honest guest/locked state (no fake sessions).

# NEON_AUTH_BASE_URL=https://ep-xxx.neonauth.<region>.aws.neon.tech/neondb/auth
# NEON_AUTH_COOKIE_SECRET=<openssl rand -base64 32>

Migrations (this round's baseline; nothing applied remotely yet):
- v2/neon/migrations/0001_app_current_baseline.sql
- applies with `neon db sql` from the Neon CLI or psql — owner-approval step,
  on a DEV branch first (see docs/launch-readiness re: branching).
- the old supabase/migrations folder is historical material; do not execute.

## Age verification vendor (round 2D)

Empty both = built-in stub stays (local `npm run dev` needs no vendor).
Set BOTH to switch the same gate UI to a real third-party verifier. The
vendor hosts any document/selfie/card flow; this site only posts a token.

# AGE_VENDOR_API_URL=https://<vendor>/v1/verify
# AGE_VENDOR_API_KEY=<server bearer key from the verifier>

## External agent keys (round 2E)

Two independent server-side credentials; scope is enforced per operation:
the drafts key covers research/drafts/affiliate metadata (never publish);
the publish key covers publish/unpublish/rollback (never draft). Keys are
sent as `Authorization: Bearer` to /api/agent/* (MCP + JSON). Never in the
browser, never in URLs, never in logs.

# AGENT_DRAFT_KEY=<random 32+ chars, server-only>
# AGENT_PUBLISH_KEY=<random 32+ chars, server-only, different from drafts>
# AGENT_DEV_STORE=memory   # DEV ONLY: local in-memory agent store; remove in prod

## Model ladder + Concierge (round 2F)

Both bases are OpenAI-compatible chat completions.
Official sources (accessed 2026-11-06):
  OneProvider base https://api.oneprovider.dev/v1 — https://oneprovider.dev/docs
  and https://oneprovider.dev/docs/api/models
  Venice base      https://api.venice.ai/api/v1   — https://docs.venice.ai/
  and https://docs.venice.ai/models/overview
Model IDs are NOT invented in code: set the CSVs below from the official
model lists; IDs missing from the provider's actual /models list are
disabled automatically at runtime.

# MODELS_MAIN_API_URL=https://api.oneprovider.dev/v1
# MODELS_MAIN_API_KEY=<key>
# MODELS_MAIN_IDS=<best-first CSV of prose model ids from the official list>
# MODELS_MAIN_COSTS_USD_PER_MTOK=<CSV parallel to IDS; output $/1M tokens; empty = unknown>
# MODELS_FALLBACK_API_URL=https://api.venice.ai/api/v1
# MODELS_FALLBACK_API_KEY=<key>
# MODELS_FALLBACK_IDS=<fallback prose model id (uncensored), CSV>
# MODELS_FALLBACK_COSTS_USD_PER_MTOK=<CSV parallel to fallback ids>

Concierge limits (server-side enforcement; empty = defaults):
# CONCIERGE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3        # 1–4 attempts per turn across the ladder
# CONCIERGE_TIMEOUT_MS=45000      # bounded overall turn timeout
# CONCIERGE_SESSION_MAX_TURNS=30  # per browser/day
# CONCIERGE_TOKEN_CEILING=1200    # max_tokens per call
# CONCIERGE_CONCURRENCY=3         # global in-flight cap
# MODEL_DAILY_COST_CAP_USD=5.00   # global daily spend cap (0 = token-only)
# CONCIERGE_DEV_MOCK=1            # DEV ONLY: labeled fixture answers; never prod

## Amazon SES mail (round 2D)

Empty region/keys/from = subscribe and contact forms report "not configured"
honestly. With values set, both send plain-text email only — no HTML blasts.
MAIL_FROM should be a verified SES identity.

# MAIL_REGION=us-east-1
# MAIL_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<iam access key id, server-only>
# MAIL_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<iam secret access key, server-only>
# MAIL_FROM="PulseNSFW" <news@pulsensfw.com>
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=<optional reply-to, usually the list inbox>
# NEWS_TO=<desk inbox for contact mail plumbed through the same helper>

## R2 media uploads (round 2E — owner-provisioned)

Storage lands in an R2 bucket through a Workers binding named `MEDIA_R2`
(wrangler.json `[r2_buckets]` binding; names documented here). The metadata
seam records source, credit, license, explicit flag and 2257 reference; the
output gate refuses explicit uploads without a 2257 reference. In dev, with
no binding, uploads return "not deployed" honestly and the plate generator
keeps covers working.

# MEDIA_R2_ACCOUNT_ID=<cloudflare account id>
# MEDIA_R2_BUCKET=<r2 bucket name for site media>
# MEDIA_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<r2 token id, server-only>
# MEDIA_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<r2 token secret, server-only>
